<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Korean Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1897 CE – 1910 CE · capital Hanseong (Seoul)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Korea's last independent monarchy, proclaimed by King Gojong in 1897 to assert equality with China and Japan, and annexed by Japan in 1910.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> in 1895 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea's</a> tributary ties, Japanese agents murdered Queen Min, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">King Gojong</a> took refuge in the Russian legation in 1896. Returning to his palace in 1897, he proclaimed the Great Han Empire and took the title of emperor, choosing the era name Gwangmu.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwangmu-reform">Gwangmu reforms</a> carried out land surveys, issued land titles and brought electricity, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, a tramway and the first railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, while strengthening imperial power. The emperor suppressed the reformist Independence Club in 1898. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> became a prize in rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and Japan's victory in the war of 1904 and 1905 left it exposed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905">Eulsa Treaty</a> of 1905 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> under Resident-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Ito Hirobumi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Gojong</a> sent secret envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1907 and was forced to abdicate for his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunjong-of-korea">Sunjong</a>; the army was disbanded, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> annexed Korea in 1910.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/korean-empire.jpg" alt="Korean Empire" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Lumia1234 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Korea_(1899).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1897 CE – 1910 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanseong (Seoul)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gojong, Sunjong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Daehan Jeguk, Empire of Korea, Great Han Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1904&amp;focus=korean-empire" aria-label="Korean Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7853 827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Korean Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8173 1083L8172 1082M8173 1083L8529 1145M8173 1083L8172 1082"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8172 1082h0M8529 1145h0M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8173 1083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8173 1083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1904&amp;focus=korean-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
